{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_c7d0f2434f464c4c9309c95d","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The `req` is deserialized via `commandObject.Command.Data.MarshalInto(ctx, &req)` — cqrs's marshaling. The cqrs layer serializes/deserializes with JSON typically. So incoming frontmatter numeric values from Kafka JSON would be float64 when decoded into `map[string]interface{}` (encoding/json default behavior).\n\nHmm wait — but actually, `lib.Task` from the agent is published over Kafka. The agents build the frontmatter map in their own language and JSON-serialize it. The controller's cqrs `MarshalInto` decodes JSON. Standard `encoding/json` decodes JSON numbers into `float64` for `interface{}` targets. So `req.Frontmatter[\"trigger_count\"]` would be `float64(1)` in production.\n\nMeanwhile the on-disk frontmatter is parsed with `yaml.Unmarshal` into `lib.TaskFrontmatter` (map[string]interface{}). yaml.v3 decodes `trigger_count: 1` into... let me verify. yaml.v3 decodes integer scalars into `int` when the target is `interface{}`. Yes — yaml.v3 uses `int` for integers (this is a well-known difference from yaml.v2 which uses `int` too actually — yaml.v2 uses int, yaml.v3 uses int; JSON uses float64).\n\nWait, actually let me double check yaml.v3: for `map[string]interface{}`, yaml.v3 decodes integer YAML values as `int`. And existing code relies on `lib.TaskFrontmatter.TriggerCount()` handling both `int` and `float64` (the accessor switches on both). The comments say: \"Int reads an integer field by key, accepting both int (JSON-decoded) and float64 (YAML-decoded) underlying types.\" — so in practice both happen.\n\nSo a raw `!=` comparison between `float64(1)` (incoming) and `int(1)` (on-disk) would be FALSE inequality → spurious decision log on every steady-state publish. This would violate DB 6 (\"Equal values produce no log line, so a steady-state publish is silent\").\n\nSo the equality comparison MUST be type-tolerant. The robust approach: compare typed values via the existing accessors, OR normalize numeric comparisons.\n\nBut the controllerOwnedFields list is just field-name strings — the merge helper needs field-specific comparison. Options:\n\nOption A: Keep the comparison generic but type-normalizing:\n```go\nfunc guardValuesEqual(a, b any) bool {\n    // normalize numeric types int/float64/int64/uint64\n    af, aok := toFloat64(a)\n    bf, bok := toFloat64(b)\n    if aok && bok { return af == bf }\n    return a == b\n}\n```\nHmm, this is getting complicated. And `lib.TaskFrontmatter` has no exported generic numeric equality.\n\nOption B: Compare via the typed accessors specifically for the two counter fields, and via `Status()` for the status field. Since the owned-field list is fixed (trigger_count, retry_count), the merge helper can switch:\n```go\nfunc valuesDiffer(field string, existing, incoming lib.TaskFrontmatter) bool {\n    switch field {\n    case \"trigger_count\":\n        return existing.TriggerCount() != incoming.TriggerCount()\n    case \"retry_count\":\n        return existing.RetryCount() != incoming.RetryCount()\n    }\n    return existing[field] != incoming[field]\n}\n```\nThis is clean and uses existing accessors. But it's field-specific logic in a guard. The spec DB 7 says \"The set of controller-owned field names ... declared once at package level ... so adding a field later is a one-line change\". Adding a field would require adding a case here too — not strictly one-line. Hmm.\n\nOption C: Type-tolerant generic equality that handles the numeric int/float case:\n```go\nfunc reflectValueEqual(a, b any) bool {\n    ra := reflect.ValueOf(a)\n    rb := reflect.ValueOf(b)\n    // ... numeric kind normalization\n}\n```\nUses reflect — heavy but general. Adding a field later = truly one-line (just add the name to controllerOwnedFields).\n\nHmm. Which is best for the prompt? The spec's intent: DB 6 steady-state silence. The AC only tests int vs int (test fixtures). Production has float64 vs int. So the prompt should specify type-tolerant comparison.\n\nI think Option C (a small helper using reflection or a numeric-switch) is the cleanest general solution. But wait — actually, is there precedent in the codebase or lib? Let me check `lib.TaskFrontmatter` for any equality helper... The agent lib has `Int(key)` and `String(key)` accessors. No equality helper.\n\nLet me reconsider. Actually — hmm, the simplest robust approach that keeps \"one-line addition\": compare via `fmt.Sprint` after numeric normalization. Actually let me think about what \"equal\" means per field:\n- trigger_count / retry_count: numeric equality (int/float64 tolerant).\n- status: the decision is about the raw on-disk vs raw incoming status values. Should `done` (incoming alias) vs `completed` (on-disk) be \"equal\"? DB 3 says terminal is decided by the normalized accessor, and the pin keeps the on-disk value. The decision log fires when \"the guard discards an incoming value that differs from the value kept on disk\". If on-disk is `completed` and incoming is `done` (alias), the pin keeps `completed` and discards `done` — they normalize to the same terminal status. Is that \"equal\" (no log) or \"different\" (log)? The AC doesn't test this. Per DB 6's letter (\"differs from the value kept on disk\"), a raw-string difference → log. But the status decision is inherently about normalized semantics...\n\nLet me keep it simple and consistent with the AC fixtures: for status, compare the normalized `Status()` values. `existing.Status() != incoming.Status()` — `completed` vs `done` → both normalize to `completed` → equal → no decision. And `completed` vs `in_progress` → different → decision. That's actually cleaner and matches \"terminal decided by normalizing accessor\". But wait — the decision's \"kept/rejected\" values should be the raw on-disk/incoming values for logging (the AC: \"the kept value 3 and the rejected value 1\"; for status the Rung-3 log names \"a field, and both values\" — raw values make sense for operators).\n\nHmm, let me reconsider — for the status decision, what does the log report? \"kept %v rejected %v\" where kept = on-disk raw status, rejected = incoming raw status. And the equality that decides whether to log uses normalized Status(). That's a reasonable, defensible design.\n\nBut actually — I realize I should double-check: does the AC test the status decision logging? No — AC 6 (DB 6) only tests trigger_count equal/differing. The status decision log isn't unit-tested in the ACs. So I have latitude. But I should still specify it coherently in the prompt.\n\nLet me settle on the cleanest coherent design:\n\n**GuardDecision struct**: `{Field string; Kept any; Rejected any}` (exported fields since external test inspects them).\n\n**MergeFrontmatter(existing, incoming lib.TaskFrontmatter) (lib.TaskFrontmatter, []GuardDecision)**:\n1. Build merged: existing base, incoming overrides.\n2. Controller-owned counters: for each field in `controllerOwnedFields`:\n   - `diskValue, onDisk := existing[field]`\n   - if `!onDisk`: if incoming carries it, `delete(merged, field)` (incoming can never introduce the key). No decision.\n   - else: `merged[field] = diskValue`. If incoming carries it AND typed values differ → append decision.\n3. Terminal status pin: if `isTerminalStatus(existing.Status())`:\n   - `merged[\"status\"] = existing[\"status\"]` (raw on-disk value, only if present).\n   - if incoming carries status AND `existing.Status() != incoming.Status()` (normalized) → append decision {Field: \"status\", Kept: existing[\"status\"], Rejected: incoming[\"status\"]}.\n4. Return.\n\n**Typed numeric comparison** for counters: I'll define a small helper. To keep it general (one-line field addition) and robust, I'll use a numeric-tolerant comparison:\n\n```go\nfunc frontmatterValueEqual(a, b any) bool {\n    if a == b {\n        return true\n    }\n    // yaml.v3 decodes ints as int, encoding/json decodes