c's whole point is \"Equal values produce no log line\". So numeric tolerance is required for correctness. I'll specify it.\n\nActually wait — let me double-check that incoming frontmatter numbers are float64 in prod. The cqrs `MarshalInto` — what does it use? Let me check cqrs base command marshal. Actually the simpler check: the agent lib's accessor comment says \"accepting both int (JSON-decoded) and float64 (YAML-decoded)\". Wait, that says int is JSON-decoded?! Let me re-read: \"Int reads an integer field by key, accepting both int (JSON-decoded) and float64 (YAML-decoded) underlying types.\" Hmm — so in their experience, JSON decode gives int and YAML decode gives float64?! That's the opposite of standard encoding/json behavior. \n\nWait, that comment might be from a different context. Let me look at how the Kafka/JSON decode works in this repo. cqrs `MarshalInto` — let me check the cqrs library's Data marshaling. Actually, `cdb.CommandObject.Data` — in cqrs, commands are serialized with a custom JSON encoder that might preserve ints (e.g., using `json.Number` or a custom marshaler). Let me check.\n\nActually — the key insight: the on-disk YAML parse. Does yaml.v3 give int or float64 for `trigger_count: 3`? Let me just test it directly. And let me check the cqrs marshaler.","signature":""}],"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9dd1ef7ac5355a1e","session_id":"d5b7e577-b8e1-45e9-9e5a-be2299ed6cef","uuid":"a4b447ed-a5a0-4a16-9bfc-ee6d38d2e760","timestamp":"2026-08-31T11:53:26.970Z","subagent_type":"dark-factory:prompt-creator","task_description":"Generate prompts from spec 006"}
