{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_861b19075b5349cfb469dbed","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Only `main.go:166` calls `NewResultWriter`, and `pkg/command/task_result_executor_test.go` uses `mocks.ResultWriter`. So changing `mergeFrontmatter` (internal) to exported `MergeFrontmatter` with a new return signature is fully safe — only the one internal call site in `buildResultModifyFn`.\n\nI have everything I need. Let me now look at the trigger_count context to know the file's full structure, particularly what's at lines 868-999 (needs_input) to understand the harness, and the section at 760-999. Actually, I've seen enough of the test harness patterns.\n\nLet me now plan the two prompts precisely.\n\n## Prompt 1: Field-ownership guard + terminal pin + widened early return + log + unit specs + CHANGELOG\n\n### Implementation changes to `pkg/result/result_writer.go`:\n\n1. **Package-level declarations (DB 7):**\n```go\n// controllerOwnedFields lists frontmatter keys the controller owns outright:\n// the on-disk value always wins over the agent's incoming value, and the\n// incoming value can never introduce the key when it is absent on disk.\n// Adding a controller-owned field is a one-line change here.\nvar controllerOwnedFields = []string{\n    \"trigger_count\",\n    \"retry_count\",\n}\n\n// terminalStatuses lists the task statuses that end a task's lifecycle. When\n// the on-disk status is terminal it is pinned and the agent's incoming status\n// is discarded; a pinned-terminal task short-circuits the escalation machinery.\nvar terminalStatuses = []domain.TaskStatus{\n    domain.TaskStatusCompleted,\n    domain.TaskStatusAborted,\n}\n```\n\nNeed to import `domain \"github.com/bborbe/vault-cli/pkg/domain\"`.\n\nWait — but the AC says `grep -c '\"trigger_count\"' pkg/result/result_writer.go` returns ≥1 — the string literal must appear in the package-level owned-field declaration. Good, `\"trigger_count\"` appears in controllerOwnedFields.\n\nAnd `grep -n 'controllerOwnedFields\\|terminalStatuses' pkg/result/result_writer.go` returns ≥2 lines (declaration + use). So both identifiers must be used somewhere in addition to declaration. Use: `controllerOwnedFields` in MergeFrontmatter loop; `terminalStatuses` in the isTerminalStatus check.\n\n2. **GuardDecision type + exported MergeFrontmatter:**\n```go\n// GuardDecision records a single ownership-guard discard: the guard kept the\n// on-disk value of a controller-owned field or the pinned terminal status and\n// rejected a differing incoming value.\ntype GuardDecision struct {\n    Field    string\n    Kept     any\n    Rejected any\n}\n```\n\n```go\n// MergeFrontmatter returns a new frontmatter map with all keys from existing,\n// overridden by all keys from incoming, then applies the field-ownership guard:\n// controller-owned counters take the on-disk value when present on disk and are\n// absent when not; a terminal on-disk status is pinned. The returned decisions\n// name every field whose differing incoming value was discarded (equal values\n// produce no decision). Neither input map is modified.\nfunc MergeFrontmatter(existing, incoming lib.TaskFrontmatter) (lib.TaskFrontmatter, []GuardDecision) {\n    merged := make(lib.TaskFrontmatter, len(existing)+len(incoming))\n    for k, v := range existing {\n        merged[k] = v\n    }\n    for k, v := range incoming {\n        merged[k] = v\n    }\n    var decisions []GuardDecision\n\n    for _, field := range controllerOwnedFields {\n        diskValue, onDisk := existing[field]\n        incomingValue, inIncoming := incoming[field]\n        if !onDisk {\n            if inIncoming {\n                delete(merged, field)\n            }\n            continue\n        }\n        merged[field] = diskValue\n        if inIncoming && !frontmatterValueEqual(diskValue, incomingValue) {\n            decisions = append(decisions, GuardDecision{Field: field, Kept: diskValue, Rejected: incomingValue})\n        }\n    }\n\n    if isTerminalStatus(existing.Status()) {\n        if diskStatus, onDisk := existing[\"status\"]; onDisk {\n            merged[\"status\"] = diskStatus\n            if incomingStatus, ok := incoming[\"status\"]; ok && existing.Status() != incoming.Status() {\n                decisions = append(decisions, GuardDecision{Field: \"status\", Kept: diskStatus, Rejected: incomingStatus})\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    return merged, decisions\n}\n```\n\nWait — subtle: the status decision equality. `existing.Status() != incoming.Status()` — compares normalized. If on-disk `aborted`, incoming `in_progress` → aborted != in_progress → decision. If on-disk `completed`, incoming `done` (alias) → both normalize to `completed` → equal → no decision (keeps on-disk `completed`, discards incoming `done` silently — correct per \"equal values produce no log\"). Good.\n\nBut what if incoming status is something like `\"weird\"` (unknown)? `incoming.Status()` normalizes unknown to `domain.TaskStatus(\"weird\")` (from the accessor: `return domain.TaskStatus(v)` for unknown). `aborted != weird` → decision. Fine.\n\n3. **isTerminalStatus helper:**\n```go\nfunc isTerminalStatus(s domain.TaskStatus) bool {\n    for _, t := range terminalStatuses {\n        if s == t {\n            return true\n        }\n    }\n    return false\n}\n```\n\nThis is where `terminalStatuses` is \"used\". But then grep 'controllerOwnedFields\\|terminalStatuses' would match: the declaration line + the isTerminalStatus loop line (uses terminalStatuses) + the MergeFrontmatter loop (uses controllerOwnedFields). ≥2 lines. \n\nHmm, but wait — a `switch` might be simpler than a loop:\n```go\nfunc isTerminalStatus(s domain.TaskStatus) bool {\n    for _, t := range terminalStatuses {\n        if s == t {\n            return true\n        }\n    }\n    return false\n}\n```\nOr `switch s { case domain.TaskStatusCompleted, domain.TaskStatusAborted: return true }`. But DB 7 wants the set declared ONCE as a package-level declaration. So the loop over terminalStatuses is right.\n\n4. **frontmatterValueEqual helper (numeric-tolerant):**\n```go\n// frontmatterValueEqual reports whether two frontmatter values compare equal,\n// treating int/float64 numeric values as equal so a JSON-decoded incoming\n// counter (float64) equals a YAML-decoded on-disk counter (int) at steady\n// state — equal counters must produce no guard log line.\nfunc frontmatterValueEqual(a, b any) bool {\n    if a == b {\n        return true\n    }\n    af, aOK := numericValue(a)\n    bf, bOK := numericValue(b)\n    return aOK && bOK && af == bf\n}\n\nfunc numericValue(v any) (float64, bool) {\n    switch n := v.(type) {\n    case int:\n        return float64(n), true\n    case int64:\n        return float64(n), true\n    case float64:\n        return n, true\n    default:\n        return 0, false\n    }\n}\n```\n\nHmm — wait. This is added complexity. Is numeric tolerance really needed, or does the test harness / prod confirm? Let me think about the risk of NOT having it: prod steady-state would log spurious decisions on every publish (float64(3) != int(3)). The Rung-3 AC would still pass (≥1 line). But DB 6 promise broken — and the spec explicitly says \"Equal values produce no log line, so a steady-state publish is silent.\" The spec is explicit. So numeric tolerance is needed. I'll include it. This is justified by the spec, not YAGNI.\n\nActually, wait — let me reconsider whether the incoming truly is float64. `commandObject.Command.Data.MarshalInto(ctx, &req)` — let me actually check cqrs's MarshalInto implementation to be sure it's encoding/json. Let me check the cqrs base package.\n\nActually the accessor comment in agent lib says \"int (JSON-decoded) and float64 (YAML-decoded)\" — which suggests in THIS system, JSON decode yields int and YAML yields float64?! That contradicts my yaml.v3 test (yaml gives int). Unless the YAML in the vault is decoded somewhere with yaml.v2 (