he section must contain the words `Controller-owned` and `trigger_count` (AC 17 greps) and `terminal` (AC 18 grep).\n   - The illustrative merge example must no longer imply blanket agent precedence — the example should show a controller-owned field surviving (e.g., disk trigger_count 5 vs incoming 1 → 5) or a terminal status pinned.\n   - Document the terminal short-circuit: a terminal on-disk status skips the cap/assignee machinery (no Trigger Cap / Retry Escalation section appended, assignee not cleared, spawn_notification preserved, phase not restored).\n3. No Go code changes; keep the section between `## Frontmatter Merge` and `## Terminal Task Status (create-task dedup)`.\n\nLet me draft the docs content the prompt should produce:\n\n```\n## Frontmatter Merge\n\nWhen writing a result back, the ResultWriter merges frontmatter from the existing task file with frontmatter provided by the agent. Agent-owned keys (phase, arbitrary frontmatter keys the agent introduces, result fields) keep today's semantics: existing keys are preserved and agent keys override on conflict. This ensures fields like `assignee`, `tags`, and `task_identifier` survive result writeback even though agents don't receive frontmatter.\n\nTwo field classes are controller-owned and the on-disk value always wins over the incoming value:\n\n| Ownership | Fields | Rule |\n|---|---|---|\n| Controller-owned | `trigger_count`, `retry_count` | On-disk value always wins; an incoming value can never introduce a key absent on disk. |\n| Controller-owned (terminal pin) | `status` | A terminal on-disk status (`completed` or `aborted`) is pinned and the incoming status is discarded; the write is a pin, not a freeze — `phase`, agent result fields, and the body still land. |\n| Agent-owned | everything else | Incoming value wins on conflict (unchanged). |\n\n```\nExisting file:  {status: aborted, trigger_count: 5, phase: ai_review}\nAgent provides: {status: in_progress, trigger_count: 1, phase: execution}\nMerged result:  {status: aborted, trigger_count: 5, phase: execution}\n```\n\nA terminal on-disk status also short-circuits the escalation machinery: no `## Trigger Cap Escalation` / `## Retry Escalation` section is appended, `assignee` is not cleared, `previous_assignee` is not written, `phase` is not restored by `restoreExistingPhase`, and an inherited `spawn_notification: true` key survives the write. Escalation exists to park a live runaway task; a task an operator already ended is not that. When the guard discards an incoming value that differs from the kept on-disk value, the writer emits an unconditional `ownership guard kept on-disk` INFO log naming the task, field, and both values; equal values produce no log line. The only mechanism that may lower a controller-owned counter is the scanner's Empty-to-Named Reset (`## Empty-to-Named Reset`), which writes `trigger_count: 0` / `retry_count: 0` to disk.\n```\n\nThis satisfies:\n- `trigger_count` in section ✓\n- `Controller-owned` in section ✓\n- `terminal` in section ✓\n- Example no longer implies blanket agent precedence ✓\n- Terminal short-circuit documented ✓\n\nNow, one concern: the docs section must remain valid — the `## Terminal Task Status (create-task dedup)` heading must still match the sed range end. Fine.\n\nLet me now write the two prompt files. I'll use the frontmatter:\n```\n---\nspec: [\"006-bug-frontmatter-field-ownership\"]\nstatus: draft\ncreated: \"2026-08-31T11:56:38Z\"\n---\n```\n\nFilenames with execution-order prefix: `1-spec-006-field-ownership-guard.md`, `2-spec-006-docs-frontmatter-ownership.md`.\n\nLet me write prompt 1 now. I need to be thorough but precise. Also I need to double check a few line numbers as anchors (optional):\n- `mergeFrontmatter` at result_writer.go:355\n- early return at result_writer.go:189\n- `buildResultModifyFn` merge call at result_writer.go:171\n- carved block at test:1351-1403\n- second-write incoming at test:1383-1394\n\nLet me also reconsider: should prompt