{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-923c8fcaa605d105","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Controller Design (task/controller)\n2\t\n3\tThe controller is the single writer to the vault git repo. It has two responsibilities: detecting task changes in git and publishing them to Kafka, and consuming commands from Kafka and writing results back to git. It has no K8s API access.\n4\t\n5\t## Inputs / Outputs\n6\t\n7\t| Direction | Topic | Purpose |\n8\t|-----------|-------|---------|\n9\t| Produces | `agent-task-v1-event` | Task created or status changed in git |\n10\t| Consumes | `agent-task-v1-request` | Update task commands (from agents) |\n11\t| Produces | `agent-task-v1-result` | Command processing confirmation (CQRS auto) |\n12\t\n13\t## Core Logic\n14\t\n15\t### 1. Change Detection (git → Kafka)\n16\t\n17\t```\n18\tPoll loop:\n19\t  │\n20\t  ├── Pull() — no-op (git-rest handles pulls internally)\n21\t  ├── gitClient.ListFiles(taskDir/*.md) → enumerate task files via HTTP\n22\t  ├── sha256-hash each file's content\n23\t  ├── compare with previous hashes\n24\t  │\n25\t  ├── changed file → parse frontmatter + body → publish agent-task-v1-event\n26\t  └── deleted file → publish agent-task-v1-event (deleted)\n27\t\n28\t> The scanner increments `agent_controller_vault_scanner_skipped_files_total{reason=<closed enum>}` at every skip site (broken frontmatter, unreadable file, empty status, injection failure, unresolvable duplicate frontmatter). The counter is pre-initialised at zero for every reason label so dashboards see all five before the first skip. Operators alert on `rate(agent_controller_vault_scanner_skipped_files_total[5m]) > 0`; a positive rate means a broken file is currently in the vault and is not being scanned.\n29\t\n30\t### 2. Command Processing (Kafka → git)\n31\t\n32\t```\n33\tOn agent-task-v1-request (operation: \"update\"):\n34\t  │\n35\t  ├── deserialize lib.Task from command payload\n36\t  ├── validate: TaskIdentifier and Content must be non-empty\n37\t  │\n38\t  ├── walk task directory, find file matching task_identifier in frontmatter\n39\t  ├── merge frontmatter + apply escalation check (counter set by executor, not incremented here)\n40\t  │     ├── read retry_count from merged frontmatter (set by executor at spawn time, spec 011)\n41\t  │     ├── if trigger_count >= max_triggers → clear assignee: \"\", preserve lifecycle phase, append ## Trigger Cap Escalation (once)\n42\t  │     ├── if retry_count >= max_retries   → clear assignee: \"\", preserve lifecycle phase, append ## Retry Escalation (once)\n43\t  │     └── if agent emits needs_input → clear assignee: \"\" (phase unchanged; spec-039 supersedes spec-021 for this row)\n44\t  ├── sanitize content (escape bare --- lines to prevent YAML corruption)\n45\t  ├── write frontmatter + content to file\n46\t  ├── git add + commit + push\n47\t  └── CQRS framework publishes success/failure result to agent-task-v1-result\n48\t```\n49\t\n50\tThe controller reads a required `VAULT_NAME` env var (CLI flag `--vault-name`) at startup naming the single Obsidian vault it serves. Every CreateCommand is checked against `VAULT_NAME` via the `pkg/routing.ShouldProcess` predicate: the effective target is `cmd.targetVault` if non-empty, otherwise the legacy fallback `openclaw`; commands whose effective target is not `VAULT_NAME` are skipped without side effects (no git write, no result publish, no error) and emit a single `glog.V(2)` line naming the command's `targetVault`, the effective target, and `VAULT_NAME` so operators can confirm routing decisions. Two controllers (e.g. one per vault) can therefore share the `agent-task-v1-request` topic without duplicating task materializations. The `targetVault` field is added to `task.CreateCommand` with `omitempty`; legacy producers that emit no `targetVault` continue to flow to the `openclaw` controller.\n51\t\n52\t## Frontmatter Merge\n53\t\n54\tWhen writing a result back, the ResultWriter merges frontmatter from the existing task file with frontmatter provided by the agent. Existing keys are preserved, agent keys override on conflict. This ensures fields like `assignee`, `tags`, and `task_identifier` survive result writeback even though agents don't receive frontmatter.\n55\t\n56\t```\n57\tExisting file:  {assignee: backtest-agent, tags: [agent-task], task_identifier: xyz}\n58\tAgent provides: {status: completed, phase: done}\n59\tMerged result:  {assignee: backtest-agent, tags: [agent-task], task_identifier: xyz, status: completed, phase: done}\n60\t```\n61\t\n62\t## Terminal Task Status (create-task dedup)\n63\t\n64\tThe vault has exactly two terminal task statuses: `completed` and `aborted`. A task in a terminal status is finished — no longer a live duplicate — so `create-task`'s dedup rule keys on whether the title path is occupied by a *live* task rather than on file existence alone.\n65\t\n66\t`done` is a `phase` value, not a `status`, and is NOT terminal for the dedup rule: a file can carry `phase: done` while its `status` is still live (status/phase merge semantics documented in `## Frontmatter Merge`), so `phase` plays no part in the dedup decision.\n67\t\n68\t`create-task`'s dedup rule is \"the title path is occupied by a live task\":\n69\t\n70\t- a create command whose title path holds a terminal task (`completed` or `aborted`) frees the slot and materializes a fresh non-terminal task at that path — an in-place overwrite whose prior instance remains recoverable via `git show <sha>~1:<path>`, recorded by a `[agent-task-controller] reopen terminal task <id>` commit and an unconditional `create-task: reopening terminal task` INFO log;\n71\t- any non-terminal status, or any existing file whose status cannot be read (absent/empty/unknown status, missing frontmatter delimiters, unparseable YAML), holds the path and the create is dropped with `ErrTaskAlreadyExists`.\n72\t\n73\t## Assignee-Clear on Escalation (spec 021, refined by spec 039, completed by spec 042)\n74\t\n75\tEvery escalation path writes `assignee: \"\"` so the task surfaces in operator inbox.\n76\tAll four rows route through the single chokepoint `result.ClearAssigneeIfHumanReview`\n77\t(for `human_review` paths) or `result.clearAssignee` (for cap paths) in\n78\t`task/controller/pkg/result/result_writer.go`:\n79\t\n80\t| Escalation trigger | `phase` written | `assignee` written | Enforcement point |\n81\t|---|---|---|---|\n82\t| `trigger_count >= max_triggers` | unchanged (lifecycle stage preserved) | `\"\"` | `applyTriggerCap` → `clearAssignee` |\n83\t| `retry_count >= max_retries` | unchanged (lifecycle stage preserved) | `\"\"` | `applyRetryCap` → `clearAssignee` |\n84\t| Agent emits `Result.NextPhase: human_review` (legitimate handoff) | `human_review` (from `resolveNextPhase`) | `\"\"` | `applyRetryCounter` → `ClearAssigneeIfHumanReview` |\n85\t| Agent emits `UpdateFrontmatterCommand` with merged `phase: human_review` (spec 042) | `human_review` | `\"\"` | `buildUpdateModifyFn` → `ClearAssigneeIfHumanReview` |\n86\t\n87\tOnce a task is parked (escalation section present, `assignee: \"\"`), repeated stale agent\n88\tresult publishes are idempotent: the escalation section is not duplicated, the lifecycle\n89\tphase is restored from the on-disk value, and assignee stays empty.\n90\t\n91\tThe `phase == \"human_review\"` assignee-clear guard in `resultWriter.applyRetryCounter`\n92\truns BEFORE the `spawn_notification` early return. This ordering is load-bearing: on\n93\ta pr-reviewer agent's first post-spawn write, the merged frontmatter carries\n94\t`spawn_notification: true` (inherited from the executor's spawn-time\n95\t`UpdateFrontmatterCommand`) AND incoming `phase: human_review` (from\n96\t`Result.NextPhase` via `resolveNextPhase`). The guard fires regardless of\n97\t`spawn_notification` state — see spec 041 for the 2026-05-25 prod incident reproducer\n98\tand prompt 075 for the same reorder pattern applied to `applyTriggerCap` on\n99\t2026-04-24.\n100\t\n101\t## Empty-to-Named Reset (spec 021)\n102\t\n103\tWhen the vault scanner observes a task file whose `assignee` transitions from empty (or absent) to a non-empty agent name, it writes `trigger_count: 0` and `retry_count: 0` back to the file atomically and queues a git commit. This refills the per-attempt budgets for the re-delegated agent without requiring manual counter edits. The reset fires exactly once per empty-to-named transition (named→named and named→empty transitions do not trigger a reset).\n104\t\n105\t## Atomic Frontmatter Commands\n106\t\n107\tIn addition to the `\"update\"` operation (full result write), the controller handles two atomic frontmatter operations on `agent-task-v1-request`:\n108\t\n109\t### `\"increment-frontmatter\"` (IncrementFrontmatterExecutor)\n110\t\n111\tPayload: `lib.IncrementFrontmatterCommand{TaskIdentifier, Field, Delta}`\n112\t\n113\t```\n114\tOn agent-task-v1-request (operation: \"increment-frontmatter\"):\n115\t  │\n116\t  ├── deserialize IncrementFrontmatterCommand\n117\t  ├── find task file by task_identifier (WalkDir)\n118\t  ├── if not found → log warning, return nil (no error)\n119\t  ├── AtomicReadModifyWriteAndCommitPush:\n120\t  │     ├── read current file bytes (under mutex)\n121\t  │     ├── parse frontmatter, read Field value (default 0 if absent)\n122\t  │     ├── newVal = currentVal + Delta\n123\t  │     ├── set Field = newVal\n124\t  │     ├── cap escalation: if Field == \"trigger_count\" AND newVal >= max_triggers\n125\t  │     │     └── clear assignee in the same write (phase unchanged; spec-039 supersedes spec-021 for this row)\n126\t  │     ├── write updated file (under mutex)\n127\t  │     └── git commit + push (under mutex)\n128\t  └── increment FrontmatterCommandsTotal{operation, outcome}\n129\t```\n130\t\n131\tDelta may be negative (decrement). Cap escalation only fires for `trigger_count` reaching `max_triggers`.\n132\t\n133\t### `\"update-frontmatter\"` (UpdateFrontmatterExecutor)\n134\t\n135\tPayload: `lib.UpdateFrontmatterCommand{TaskIdentifier, Updates map[string]any}`\n136\t\n137\t```\n138\tOn agent-task-v1-request (operation: \"update-frontmatter\"):\n139\t  │\n140\t  ├── deserialize UpdateFrontmatterCommand\n141\t  ├── if Updates is empty → return nil (no-op, no write)\n142\t  ├── find task file by task_identifier (WalkDir)\n143\t  ├── if not found → log warning, return nil\n144\t  ├── AtomicReadModifyWriteAndCommitPush:\n145\t  │     ├── read current file bytes (under mutex)\n146\t  │     ├── parse existing frontmatter\n147\t  │     ├── merge only the keys in Updates (all other keys unchanged)\n148\t  │     ├── if Body section provided → append/replace section in body (spec 016)\n149\t  │     ├── if merged phase == \"human_review\" → result.ClearAssigneeIfHumanReview clears assignee in the same write (spec 042)\n150\t  │     ├── write updated file (under mutex)\n151\t  │     └── git commit + push (under mutex)\n152\t  └── increment FrontmatterCommandsTotal{operation, outcome}\n153\t```\n154\t\n155\t## Vault Writes via git-rest\n156\t\n157\tThe controller holds no local git clone. All vault file operations flow through the\n158\t`vault-obsidian-openclaw` git-rest StatefulSet via HTTP:\n159\t\n160\t| Operation | HTTP call | Who commits |\n161\t|-----------|-----------|-------------|\n162\t| Read file | `GET /api/v1/files/{relPath}` | N/A |\n163\t| Write file | `POST /api/v1/files/{relPath}` | git-rest (auto-commit) |\n164\t| Delete file | `DELETE /api/v1/files/{relPath}` | git-rest (auto-commit) |\n165\t| List files | `GET /api/v1/files/?glob={pattern}` | N/A |\n166\t\n167\tgit-rest ensures one commit per write. The controller's `/readiness` endpoint reflects\n168\tgit-rest readiness: if git-rest returns 503 (push stuck), the controller reports 503\n169\tand the Kafka consumer goroutine blocks inside the write retry loop until git-rest\n170\trecovers. Kafka offsets are not advanced during this block.\n171\t\n172\tBoltDB (at `/data/bolt` on the `datadir` PVC) continues to track Kafka consumer\n173\toffsets — unchanged from the pre-migration architecture.\n174\t\n175\t## Content Sanitization\n176\t\n177\tAgent output may contain bare `---` lines that would corrupt YAML frontmatter boundaries. The ResultWriter escapes these to `\\-\\-\\-` before writing.\n178\t\n179\t## HTTP Endpoints\n180\t\n181\t| Endpoint | Purpose |\n182\t|----------|---------|\n183\t| `/healthz` | Liveness probe |\n184\t| `/readiness` | Readiness probe |\n185\t| `/metrics` | Prometheus metrics |\n186\t| `/setloglevel` | Temporary log level change (5-min auto-reset) |\n187\t| `/trigger` | On-demand vault scan cycle |\n188\t\n189\t## What the Controller Does NOT Do\n190\t\n191\t- No K8s API calls (task/executor handles job spawning)\n192\t- No domain logic (doesn't know what a backtest is)\n193\t- No job management (doesn't know about pods)\n194\t- No prompt conversion (removed in v0.17.0)\n195\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-84189248c2963a46","session_id":"d5b7e577-b8e1-45e9-9e5a-be2299ed6cef","uuid":"cf1d5c2a-16d0-4c5b-a525-c1a272190d18","timestamp":"2026-08-31T12:04:10.872Z","subagent_type":"dark-factory:prompt-auditor","task_description":"Audit prompt 2-spec-006-docs-frontmatter-ownership"}
