{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-979cec8725233846","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tspec: [\"006-bug-frontmatter-field-ownership\"]\n3\tstatus: draft\n4\tcreated: \"2026-08-31T11:56:38Z\"\n5\t---\n6\t\n7\t# Field-ownership guard, terminal pin, guard log, unit specs, changelog\n8\t\n9\t<summary>\n10\t\n11\t- A result publish can no longer roll back `trigger_count` / `retry_count`: the on-disk counter always wins, and an incoming payload can never introduce a controller-owned counter that is absent on disk — so the trigger and retry caps compare against real spawn counts and can fire.\n12\t- A terminal on-disk `status` (`aborted` / `completed`) is pinned: the incoming `status` is discarded, so an operator's abort survives every publish.\n13\t- A pinned-terminal task short-circuits the escalation machinery uniformly for both terminal statuses: no Trigger Cap / Retry Escalation section, no assignee clear, no `previous_assignee`, no phase restore, and an inherited `spawn_notification: true` survives — escalation exists to park a live task, not one an operator already ended.\n14\t- Everything the agent legitimately authors — `phase`, arbitrary keys, the body — keeps today's incoming-wins behavior on terminal and non-terminal tasks alike.\n15\t- Every guard discard of a differing incoming value emits one unconditional INFO log line containing `ownership guard kept on-disk` (task, field, kept value, rejected value); equal values stay silent, so steady-state publishes produce no log noise.\n16\t- The controller-owned field names and the terminal status set are each declared once at package level, so adding a controller-owned field later is a one-line change.\n17\t- The one pre-existing spec that models an incoming counter reset is rewritten to model the reset the way the system actually performs it — the scanner's Empty-to-Named Reset writing `trigger_count: 0` to disk — while preserving its intent (`previous_assignee` persistence, `assignee: backtest-agent`).\n18\t- New unit specs prove: counters survive, absent counters are never introduced, the cap fires because the on-disk counter won, terminal statuses pin, escalation short-circuits, the merge reports exactly the right guard decisions, and agent-owned fields still win on non-terminal tasks.\n19\t- The changelog records the fix under `## Unreleased` naming the counter rollback and the reverted terminal status.\n20\t\n21\t</summary>\n22\t\n23\t<objective>\n24\t\n25\tMake the single result-write chokepoint (`pkg/result`) field-ownership-aware: `trigger_count` / `retry_count` always take the on-disk value (and are never introduced from the incoming payload), a terminal on-disk `status` is pinned and discards the incoming status, the escalation early return covers both terminal statuses, every differing-value discard emits one unconditional `ownership guard kept on-disk` INFO log line, and all of it is proven by unit specs in `pkg/result/result_writer_test.go`. Record the behavior change in `CHANGELOG.md`.\n26\t\n27\t</objective>\n28\t\n29\t<context>\n30\t\n31\tThere is no CLAUDE.md in this repo; the global YOLO container CLAUDE.md (already in your context) governs project conventions. Read the repo's own code as the source of truth for style.\n32\t\n33\tRead the coding-plugin docs that apply to this change:\n34\t- `/home/node/.claude/plugins/marketplaces/coding/docs/go-glog-guide.md` (unconditional `glog.Infof` vs `glog.V(n).Infof`)\n35\t- `/home/node/.claude/plugins/marketplaces/coding/docs/go-testing-guide.md` (Ginkgo/Gomega, `DescribeTable`, coverage ≥80% for changed code)\n36\t- `/home/node/.claude/plugins/marketplaces/coding/docs/go-error-wrapping-guide.md`\n37\t- `/home/node/.claude/plugins/marketplaces/coding/docs/definition-of-done.md`\n38\t- `/home/node/.claude/plugins/marketplaces/coding/docs/changelog-guide.md`\n39\t\n40\tRead `pkg/result/result_writer.go` IN FULL (15309 bytes). The functions you change:\n41\t- `func mergeFrontmatter(existing, incoming lib.TaskFrontmatter) lib.TaskFrontmatter` — currently at the bottom of the file (around line 353-364); the ONLY call site is `buildResultModifyFn` (line 171), so changing its signature is safe. It is the result-write chokepoint this spec targets.\n42\t- `func (r *resultWriter) buildResultModifyFn(ctx context.Context, req lib.Task) func(current []byte) ([]byte, error)` (line 151) — the modify callback re-reads on-disk frontmatter per git retry and calls the merge. This is where the guard log lines are emitted (it holds `req.TaskIdentifier`).\n43\t- `func (r *resultWriter) applyRetryCounter(merged, existing lib.TaskFrontmatter, body string) string` (line 188) — its terminal early return is currently `if string(merged.Status()) == \"completed\" { return body }` (line 189).\n44\t- `applyTriggerCap`, `applyRetryCap`, `clearAssignee`, `ClearAssigneeIfHumanReview`, `restoreExistingPhase` — NOT changed by this spec; they simply become unreachable for pinned-terminal tasks because the widened early return fires first.\n45\t\n46\tVerified library facts (grep-checked against module sources; do not re-derive):\n47\t- `type TaskFrontmatter map[string]interface{}` from `github.com/bborbe/agent` (imported as `lib`).\n48\t- `func (f TaskFrontmatter) Status() domain.TaskStatus` — the normalizing accessor; canonicalises aliases via `domain.NormalizeTaskStatus` (e.g. `done` → `completed`), and returns `domain.TaskStatus(v)` for unknown values. Terminal detection MUST use this accessor, never a raw string comparison.\n49\t- `domain.TaskStatusCompleted TaskStatus = \"completed\"` and `domain.TaskStatusAborted TaskStatus = \"aborted\"` from `github.com/bborbe/vault-cli/pkg/domain` (already a direct require in `go.mod`; `result_writer.go` does NOT yet import it — add the import).\n50\t- `func (f TaskFrontmatter) TriggerCount() int` / `RetryCount() int` — both accept `int` and `float64` underlying values.\n51\t- `github.com/golang/glog` is already imported; `glog.Infof(format string, args ...any)` is the unconditional INFO call.\n52\t- On-disk frontmatter is parsed with `gopkg.in/yaml.v3` into `map[string]interface{}`, which decodes `trigger_count: 3` as `int(3)`; the incoming `req.Frontmatter` reaches `WriteResult` after a `json.Marshal`+`json.Unmarshal` round-trip through cqrs (`base/base_event.go MarshalInto`, standard `encoding/json`, no `UseNumber`), which decodes the same number as `float64(3)`. The guard's value-equality test MUST therefore treat `int` and `float64` as numerically equal, or every steady-state publish would log a spurious guard line, violating DB 6 (\"Equal values produce no log line, so a steady-state publish is silent\").\n53\t\n54\tRead `pkg/result/result_writer_test.go` IN FULL (1748 lines, package `result_test`, external test package). The harness you reuse:\n55\t- `BeforeEach` (line 57-105): builds `fakeGit *mocks.GitClient` with a real-filesystem `AtomicReadModifyWriteAndCommitPushStub`, `fakeTime`, `identifier`, and `writer = result.NewResultWriter(fakeGit, taskDir, fakeTime, metrics.New())`.\n56\t- `writeTaskFile := func(name, content string) string` (line 111) writes an on-disk file; `taskFile = lib.Task{...}` models the agent's incoming payload; assertions read the written file and use `ContainSubstring` / `NotTo(ContainSubstring)`.\n57\t- The existing spec `It(\"agent keys override existing keys\")` (line 186) MUST keep passing with unmodified `Expect(` lines — it is the non-terminal agent-wins control.\n58\t- The pre-existing `It(\"previous_assignee persists when operator re-delegates by setting a non-empty assignee\")` (line 1351-1403) is the ONLY existing block you may modify (the spec carve-out, see Requirement 6). Every other existing `It(`/`Expect(` in this file must remain byte-identical.\n59\t- Every existing spec that carries `trigger_count` / `retry_count` in its incoming payload already uses on-disk == incoming values, so the guard changes none of their outcomes — this is deliberate; the only incoming-carried counter reset in the file is inside the carved block.\n60\t\n61\tThe spec's Test-style directive: use `DescribeTable` for the counter rows and for the two terminal-status rows (same fixture shape, differing values), and plain `It` blocks for the pin-not-freeze, short-circuit, log (DB 6), and negative-control cases, matching the surrounding file. `DescribeTable` style precedent lives in `pkg/command/command_operations_test.go` (line 21): `DescribeTable(\"...\", func(...) {...}, Entry(\"name\", args...), ...)`.\n62\t\n63\tRead `CHANGELOG.md` — a `## Unreleased` section already exists (line 8) with one `fix:` bullet (terminal-task reopens); append a NEW `fix:` bullet to that existing section (do not create a second `## Unreleased`, do not touch the top version heading `## v0.6.1`).\n64\t\n65\tSpec cross-references to keep straight:\n66\t- DB 1-2: counters read only from disk → caps see real spawn counts.\n67\t- DB 3: terminal pin via normalizing `Status()` vs the two `domain` constants.\n68\t- DB 4: pin happens during the merge, before `applyRetryCounter`, whose early return is widened from `completed` to BOTH terminal statuses; consequence = pinned-terminal tasks never reach the cap/assignee/`spawn_notification` machinery.\n69\t- DB 5: agent-owned keys keep incoming-wins on terminal and non-terminal tasks alike.\n70\t- DB 6: the merge helper reports guard decisions to the caller; the caller emits one unconditional INFO line per decision naming task, field, kept value, rejected value; equal values → no decision → silent.\n71\t- DB 7: controller-owned field names and terminal statuses each declared once at package level.\n72\t\n73\t</context>\n74\t\n75\t<requirements>\n76\t\n77\tAll changes below are in `pkg/result/result_writer.go` unless stated otherwise.\n78\t\n79\t1. **Add the `domain` import.** Add `domain \"github.com/bborbe/vault-cli/pkg/domain\"` to the import block (already a direct `go.mod` dependency; `github.com/bborbe/agent` imports the same package, so no cycle). goimports-reviser in `make precommit` will place it; match the grouping style of the existing imports.\n80\t\n81\t2. **Add the two package-level ownership declarations (DB 7).** Place them near the top of the file, after the `resultWriter` struct (around line 52), before `FindTaskFilePath`. The set of controller-owned field names and the set of terminal statuses each live here ONCE — a future controller-owned field is a one-line addition to `controllerOwnedFields`:\n82\t   ```go\n83\t   // controllerOwnedFields lists frontmatter keys whose on-disk value always wins:\n84\t   // the result writer never lets the agent's incoming value overwrite them, and an\n85\t   // incoming value can never introduce a controller-owned key that is absent on disk.\n86\t   var controllerOwnedFields = []string{\n87\t       \"trigger_count\",\n88\t       \"retry_count\",\n89\t   }\n90\t\n91\t   // terminalStatuses lists the statuses that end a task's lifecycle. When the on-disk\n92\t   // status is terminal, the merge pins it (discarding the incoming status) and the\n93\t   // escalation machinery short-circuits. Terminal is decided via the normalizing\n94\t   // TaskFrontmatter.Status() accessor compared against these constants.\n95\t   var terminalStatuses = []domain.TaskStatus{\n96\t       domain.TaskStatusCompleted,\n97\t       domain.TaskStatusAborted,\n98\t   }\n99\t   ```\n100\t   The string literal `\"trigger_count\"` MUST appear in this declaration (spec AC 12 greps for it).\n101\t\n102\t3. **Replace `mergeFrontmatter` with the exported `MergeFrontmatter` and add the `GuardDecision` type.** Delete the existing `mergeFrontmatter` function and its doc comment (currently around line 353-364). Add the decision type and the new exported function in its place (the export is REQUIRED: `result_writer_test.go` is `package result_test`, so the DB 6 decision-reporting unit specs can only reach the helper through an exported symbol — `FindTaskFilePath` and `ExtractBody` are already exported in this package, so this matches surrounding style):\n103\t   ```go\n104\t   // GuardDecision records a single ownership-guard discard: the merge kept the\n105\t   // on-disk value of a controller-owned field (or the pinned terminal status) and\n106\t   // rejected a differing incoming value. Equal values produce no decision.\n107\t   type GuardDecision struct {\n108\t       Field    string\n109\t       Kept     any\n110\t       Rejected any\n111\t   }\n112\t\n113\t   // MergeFrontmatter returns a new frontmatter map with all keys from existing,\n114\t   // overridden by all keys from incoming, then applies the field-ownership guard:\n115\t   // controller-owned counters take the on-disk value when present on disk and are\n116\t   // absent from the result when not; a terminal on-disk status is pinned. The\n117\t   // returned decisions name every field whose differing incoming value was discarded\n118\t   // (equal values produce no decision). Neither input map is modified.\n119\t   func MergeFrontmatter(existing, incoming lib.TaskFrontmatter) (lib.TaskFrontmatter, []GuardDecision) {\n120\t       merged := make(lib.TaskFrontmatter, len(existing)+len(incoming))\n121\t       for k, v := range existing {\n122\t           merged[k] = v\n123\t       }\n124\t       for k, v := range incoming {\n125\t           merged[k] = v\n126\t       }\n127\t       var decisions []GuardDecision\n128\t\n129\t       // Controller-owned counters: the on-disk value always wins when the key exists\n130\t       // on disk (kept verbatim, whatever its type); a key absent on disk stays absent\n131\t       // even if the incoming payload carries it.\n132\t       for _, field := range controllerOwnedFields {\n133\t           diskValue, onDisk := existing[field]\n134\t           incomingValue, inIncoming := incoming[field]\n135\t           if !onDisk {\n136\t               if inIncoming {\n137\t                   delete(merged, field)\n138\t               }\n139\t               continue\n140\t           }\n141\t           merged[field] = diskValue\n142\t           if inIncoming && !frontmatterValueEqual(diskValue, incomingValue) {\n143\t               decisions = append(decisions, GuardDecision{Field: field, Kept: diskValue, Rejected: incomingValue})\n144\t           }\n145\t       }\n146\t\n147\t       // Terminal on-disk status is pinned: the on-disk status value is kept and the\n148\t       // incoming status is discarded. Terminal is decided by the normalizing Status()\n149\t       // accessor compared against terminalStatuses, never by raw string equality on\n150\t       // the unparsed value. The status decision is reported only when the normalized\n151\t       // values differ (an incoming alias such as \"done\" against on-disk \"completed\"\n152\t       // is normalized-equal and produces no decision).\n153\t       if diskStatus, onDisk := existing[\"status\"]; onDisk && isTerminalStatus(existing.Status()) {\n154\t           merged[\"status\"] = diskStatus\n155\t           if incomingStatus, ok := incoming[\"status\"]; ok && existing.Status() != incoming.Status() {\n156\t               decisions = append(decisions, GuardDecision{Field: \"status\", Kept: diskStatus, Rejected: incomingStatus})\n157\t           }\n158\t       }\n159\t\n160\t       return merged, decisions\n161\t   }\n162\t   ```\n163\t   All other keys follow today's incoming-wins merge, on terminal and non-terminal tasks alike (DB 5) — no extra branching.\n164\t\n165\t4. **Add the three package-private helpers** `isTerminalStatus`, `frontmatterValueEqual`, `numericValue` (place near `MergeFrontmatter`):\n166\t   ```go\n167\t   func isTerminalStatus(s domain.TaskStatus) bool {\n168\t       for _, t := range terminalStatuses {\n169\t           if s == t {\n170\t               return true\n171\t           }\n172\t       }\n173\t       return false\n174\t   }\n175\t\n176\t   // frontmatterValueEqual reports whether two frontmatter values compare equal,\n177\t   // treating numeric int/float64 pairs as equal so a JSON-decoded incoming counter\n178\t   // (float64) equals a YAML-decoded on-disk counter (int) at steady state — equal\n179\t   // counters must produce no guard log line (DB 6).\n180\t   func frontmatterValueEqual(a, b any) bool {\n181\t       if a == b {\n182\t           return true\n183\t       }\n184\t       af, aOK := numericValue(a)\n185\t       bf, bOK := numericValue(b)\n186\t       return aOK && bOK && af == bf\n187\t   }\n188\t\n189\t   func numericValue(v any) (float64, bool) {\n190\t       switch n := v.(type) {\n191\t       case int:\n192\t           return float64(n), true\n193\t       case int64:\n194\t           return float64(n), true\n195\t       case float64:\n196\t           return n, true\n197\t       default:\n198\t           return 0, false\n199\t       }\n200\t   }\n201\t   ```\n202\t   Do NOT coerce the on-disk counter value to an integer when keeping it: a non-integer on-disk value (e.g. the string `\"3\"` or `null`) is kept verbatim, and the cap arithmetic reads it through the existing `TriggerCount()`/`RetryCount()` accessors (which yield `0` for non-integers, so no escalation fires — unchanged from today, spec Failure-Modes row \"On-disk counter is not an integer\").\n203\t\n204\t5. **Update `buildResultModifyFn` to consume the decisions and emit the guard log.** Replace the single merge call at line 171 with the two-value return and a loop that emits one unconditional INFO line per decision. The frozen substring `ownership guard kept on-disk` MUST appear verbatim in the `glog.Infof` call, which MUST be unconditional (`glog.Infof`, NOT `glog.V(n).Infof`), naming the task identifier, the field, the kept on-disk value and the rejected incoming value (spec AC 13 + DB 6; the Rung-3 post-deploy AC greps prod logs for it at default verbosity):\n205\t   ```go\n206\t   merged, decisions := MergeFrontmatter(currentOnDisk, req.Frontmatter)\n207\t   for _, d := range decisions {\n208\t       glog.Infof(\n209\t           \"ownership guard kept on-disk: task %s field %s kept %v rejected %v\",\n210\t           req.TaskIdentifier,\n211\t           d.Field,\n212\t           d.Kept,\n213\t           d.Rejected,\n214\t       )\n215\t   }\n216\t   body := r.applyRetryCounter(merged, currentOnDisk, string(req.Content))\n217\t   ```\n218\t   The log line must name only the task identifier, the field, and the two values — NEVER the body or the full frontmatter (spec Security / Log safety). Do not change anything else in `buildResultModifyFn` (the per-retry re-read, the body-replacement semantics, and the marshal/write steps stay exactly as they are).\n219\t\n220\t6. **Widen the terminal early return in `applyRetryCounter` (DB 4).** Change:\n221\t   ```go\n222\t   if string(merged.Status()) == \"completed\" {\n223\t       return body\n224\t   }\n225\t   ```\n226\t   (currently at line 189) to:\n227\t   ```go\n228\t   if isTerminalStatus(merged.Status()) {\n229\t       return body\n230\t   }\n231\t   ```\n232\t   This is the intended control-flow change: the pin during the merge means `merged.Status()` is already terminal for a pinned-terminal task, so the early return fires before `applyTriggerCap`, `ClearAssigneeIfHumanReview`, the `spawn_notification` delete, and `applyRetryCap` — a pinned-terminal task accrues no escalation section, its `assignee` is not cleared, `previous_assignee` is not written, `phase` is not restored by `restoreExistingPhase`, and an inherited `spawn_notification: true` key survives. Both terminal statuses take one uniform control flow. Do not change any other statement in `applyRetryCounter`.\n233\t\n234\t7. **Rewrite the carved-out spec** `It(\"previous_assignee persists when operator re-delegates by setting a non-empty assignee\")` (currently line 1351-1403) in `pkg/result/result_writer_test.go`. This is the ONLY existing block you may modify. The first write (lines 1354-1377, cap fires → `previous_assignee: claude` written, `assignee` cleared) stays unchanged. Rewrite the second-write portion to model the reset the way the system actually performs it — the scanner's Empty-to-Named Reset (spec 021) writes `trigger_count: 0` to DISK on an empty→named assignee transition, it is NOT carried in the incoming payload:\n235\t   - Delete `\"trigger_count\":   0, // operator reset` from the second-write incoming `lib.Task{...}`.\n236\t   - BEFORE the second `Expect(writer.WriteResult(ctx, taskFile)).To(Succeed())`, add a `writeTaskFile(\"my-task.md\", ...)` call that rewrites the on-disk file to `status: in_progress`, `phase: planning`, `trigger_count: 0`, `max_triggers: 3`, `assignee: backtest-agent`, AND `previous_assignee: claude`, body `## Task\\nRetrying with backtest-agent.\\n`. The rewritten on-disk content MUST itself retain `previous_assignee: claude` so the persistence assertion tests persistence rather than re-creation.\n237\t   - The second-write incoming `lib.Task{...}` keeps `status: in_progress`, `phase: planning`, `max_triggers: 3`, `assignee: backtest-agent` (no `trigger_count` key).\n238\t   - Keep both existing post-write assertions unchanged: `Expect(s2).To(ContainSubstring(\"previous_assignee: claude\"))` and `Expect(s2).To(ContainSubstring(\"assignee: backtest-agent\"))`.\n239\t   The spec's intent is preserved: `previous_assignee: claude` persists (retained on disk through the reset, then preserved by the merge because the incoming payload does not carry it) and `assignee: backtest-agent` is set. With `trigger_count: 0` on disk, the second publish does not re-escalate.\n240\t\n241\t8. **Add the new unit specs to `pkg/result/result_writer_test.go`.** Do not modify any existing block other than the carve-out. Follow the spec's Test-style directive (`DescribeTable` for the counter rows and the terminal-status rows; plain `It` for the rest), and reuse the `writeTaskFile` / `lib.Task` / `ContainSubstring` harness from `BeforeEach`. Assert on the WRITTEN FILE BYTES (the