t` | `pkg/result/result_writer_test.go` | Correct |\n| Carved block `It(\"previous_assignee persists…\")` 1351–1403; first write 1355–1377; `\"trigger_count\": 0, // operator reset` at line 1389; second `Expect` 1395; assertions 1399/1401 | `pkg/result/result_writer_test.go` | Correct |\n| `Context(\"trigger_count cap escalation\")` opens 999, closes 1494 (8a insertion point) | `pkg/result/result_writer_test.go` | Correct |\n| `Context(\"atomic write and push error\")` at 1589 | `pkg/result/result_writer_test.go` | Correct (see Recommendation 1: an interleaved Context 1496–1587 sits between the two cited anchors) |\n| `Describe(\"ExtractBody\")` 1714 → closes 1747, final `})` 1748 (8c insertion point) | `pkg/result/result_writer_test.go` | Correct |\n| `## Unreleased` line 8 (one existing `fix:` bullet), first `## v` heading `## v0.6.1` line 12 | `CHANGELOG.md` | Correct; the `awk` verification exits 1 today, matching the spec's claim |\n| `type TaskFrontmatter map[string]interface{}`; `Status()` routes through `domain.NormalizeTaskStatus` and returns `domain.TaskStatus(v)` for unknown; `TriggerCount()`/`RetryCount()` accept `int` and `float64` | agent module `v0.83.0` (`agent_task-frontmatter.go`) | Correct (grep-verified) |\n| `domain.TaskStatusCompleted TaskStatus = \"completed\"`, `domain.TaskStatusAborted TaskStatus = \"aborted\"`; `NormalizeTaskStatus` maps `done`→`completed`, `aborted`→`aborted` | vault-cli `v0.116.1` `pkg/domain/task_status.go` | Correct (grep-verified) |\n| `domain` not yet imported in `result_writer.go`; direct require at `go.mod:21`; agent already imports vault-cli domain (no cycle) | `go.mod`, module sources | Correct |\n| `glog.Infof` | glog v1.2.5 (direct dep `go.mod:22`, already imported and used in `result_writer.go`) | Not locally grep-verifiable (module not in this cache), but it is the standard unconditional glog INFO API and the module is an existing direct dep — low risk |\n| `goimports-reviser` runs in `make precommit` | `Makefile.precommit:15` | Correct |\n| `make test`/`make precommit` at repo root | Single-module repo (one `go.mod`); root `Makefile` includes `Makefile.precommit` with `test:` and `precommit:` targets | Correct — not a multi-service repo, so the global \"never at root\" rule does not apply |\n| Carve-out claim that only one existing block carries differing on-disk vs incoming counter | All counter-carrying tests sampled (lines 501–1473): on-disk == incoming or no counter in incoming; the only differing case is the carve-out (line 1389) | Correct |\n| `Empty-to-Named Reset (spec 021)` referenced in Requirement 7 | `docs/controller-design.md:101` | Correct |\n\n## Coding Guidelines Compliance\n\n| Guideline | Doc | Status |\n|-----------|-----|--------|\n| Guard log is unconditional `glog.Infof`, never `glog.V(n).Infof`, names only task/field/values | `go-glog-guide.md` | Compliant (Requirements 5 + Constraints are explicit) |\n| No `fmt.Errorf`, no `context.Background()` in `pkg/` | `go-error-wrapping-guide.md` | Compliant (no new error paths added) |\n| Ginkgo/Gomega, external test package, `DescribeTable`, coverage ≥80% for changed code | `go-testing-guide.md` | Compliant |\n| No test-only package-level mutable state (`var X` + `SetX` pair) | `go-composition.md` | Compliant (`controllerOwnedFields`/`terminalStatuses` are production declarations demanded by DB 7, not test helpers) |\n| No new interfaces → no counterfeiter requirement | — | N/A |\n\n## YAGNI Pass (scope-creep detector)\n\n- [x] No opt-out flag / escape hatch for the shipped behavior (spec Non-goals explicitly forbid a config flag/env/opt-out; Constraints repeat it)\n- [x] `controllerOwnedFields`, `terminalStatuses`, `GuardDecision`, `frontmatterValueEqual`/`numericValue`, and the widened early return are each demanded by spec DBs 4/6/7\n- [x] No tunable thresholds, no \"future consumer might want…\" knobs, no test cases exercising a non-existent flag\n\nClean pass.\n\n## Critical Issues\n\n**1. The int/float64 steady-state equality contract (DB 6) has no test — the one boundary the prompt itself flags as load-bearing is unguarded.**\n\nThe prompt's own `<context>` (line 52) states: the on-disk counter is YAML-decoded as `int`, the incoming counter reaches `WriteResult` after a JSON round-trip through cqrs that decodes it as `float64`, and therefore `frontmatterValueEqual` \"MUST … treat `int` and `float64` as numerically equal, or every steady-state publish would log a spurious guard line, violating DB 6.\"\n\nYet no shipped test ever produces a `float64` counter value. In the test harness there is no JSON round-trip — incoming frontmatter is built directly as a Go literal (`lib.TaskFrontmatter{\"trigger_count\": 1}` → `int`). So:\n- 8b counter rows compare on-disk YAML `int` vs incoming Go `int`;\n- 8c's two `It`s compare `int(3)` vs `int(3)` and `int(3)` vs `int(1)`.\n\nIf `numericValue` were deleted and `frontmatterValueEqual` reduced to `a == b`, every one of the prompt's tests would still pass, while production would emit a spurious `ownership guard kept on-disk: task … field trigger_count kept 3 rejected 3` line on every steady-state publish — a DB 6 violation and log flood. This is precisely the audit's boundary-crossing pattern (a new value crosses a serialization boundary; shape tests pass; the boundary contract fails at runtime). A cheap level-1 test is available: call `result.MergeFrontmatter` with a mixed int/float64 pair.\n\n**Fix** — in Requirement 8c, inside the new `Describe(\"MergeFrontmatter\", …)`, insert the following two `It` blocks after the existing second `It` (the \"reports exactly one decision …\" block ending with `Expect(decisions[0].Rejected).To(Equal(1))` and its closing `})`), before the `Describe`'s final `})`:\n\n> OLD:\n> ```\n>            Expect(decisions[0].Field).To(Equal(\"trigger_count\"))\n>            Expect(decisions[0].Kept).To(Equal(3))\n>            Expect(decisions[0].Rejected).To(Equal(1))\n>        })\n>    })\n> ```\n>\n> NEW:\n> ```\n>            Expect(decisions[0].Field).To(Equal(\"trigger_count\"))\n>            Expect(decisions[0].Kept).To(Equal(3))\n>            Expect(decisions[0].Rejected).To(Equal(1))\n>        })\n>\n>        // DB 6 steady-state contract: the incoming counter arrives JSON-decoded as\n>        // float64 (cqrs round-trip) while the on-disk counter is YAML-decoded int.\n>        // frontmatterValueEqual's numeric normalization is the only thing preventing\n>        // a spurious guard log line on every steady-state publish — this is the\n>        // mixed-type path the equal-int row above does not exercise.\n>        It(\"reports zero guard decisions when the incoming float64 counter equals the on-disk int\", func() {\n>            existing := lib.TaskFrontmatter{\"status\": \"in_progress\", \"trigger_count\": 3}\n>            incoming := lib.TaskFrontmatter{\"status\": \"in_progress\", \"trigger_count\": float64(3)}\n>            _, decisions := result.MergeFrontmatter(existing, incoming)\n>            Expect(decisions).To(HaveLen(0))\n>        })\n>\n>        It(\"reports one decision when the incoming float64 counter differs from the on-disk int\", func() {\n>            existing := lib.TaskFrontmatter{\"status\": \"in_progress\", \"trigger_count\": 5}\n>            incoming := lib.TaskFrontmatter{\"status\": \"in_progress\", \"trigger_count\": float64(1)}\n>            _, decisions := result.MergeFrontmatter(existing, incoming)\n>            Expect(decisions).To(HaveLen(1))\n>            Expect(decisions[0].Field).To(Equal(\"trigger_count\"))\n>            Expect(decisions[0].Kept).To(Equal(5))\n>            Expect(decisions[0].Rejected).To(Equal(float64(1)))\n>        })\n>    })\n> ```\n\nThis pins the exact boundary the prompt's context warns about, with no serialization harness needed.\n\n## Recommendations\n\n1. **8b placement anchor is imprecise.** Requirement 8b says to place the new `Context(\"field ownership guard\")` \"right after `Context(\"trigger_count cap escalation\")` closes and before `Context(\"atomic write and push error\")` around line 1589.\" But `Context(\"interleaved partial update between read and write (race-fix regression)\")` (lines 1496–1587) sits between those two anchors. The actual insertion point is between line 1587 (interleaved context close) and line 1589. The anchor \"before `Context(\"atomic write and push error\")`\" disambiguates it, but reword to: \"place it after `Context(\"interleaved partial update …\")` closes (line 1587) and before `Context(\"atomic write and push error\")` (line 1589).\"\n\n2. **Spec failure-mode row \"On-disk counter is not an integer\" is untested.** Requirement 4 correctly instructs \"Do NOT coerce the on-disk counter value to an integer when keeping it,\" but no test proves a non-integer on-disk value (e.g. `trigger_count: \"3\"` or `null`) is kept verbatim while the caps stay silent. A cheap 8b table row (on-disk `trigger_count: \"3\"`, incoming `trigger_count: 7`) would pin the verbatim-keep. Recommendation severity: the row is \"unchanged from today\" for the cap outcome, so a regression would be a silent drift rather than a break.\n\n3. **Document the widened-early-return side effect for incoming-terminal status on a non-terminal task.** The Requirement 6 widening also short-circuits when the *incoming* status is terminal on a non-terminal on-disk task (e.g. incoming `status: aborted` against on-disk `in_progress` — today that falls through into the cap/assignee machinery; post-fix it returns immediately). This is spec-mandated (Design Decision 2) and no existing test breaks (none carries incoming `aborted`), but the prompt describes the change only in terms of pinned-terminal tasks. One sentence noting this consequence would prevent an executor from \"fixing\" it as an unintended regression. Optionally add a test row.\n\n4. **Stale comment after the rename.** `result_writer.go:200` (inside `applyRetryCounter`'s comment block) says \"inherited it via mergeFrontmatter.\" After Requirement 3 renames to `MergeFrontmatter`, this becomes stale. The prompt freezes \"Do not change any other statement in applyRetryCounter\" — a comment-name update is cosmetic, not a statement change, so the executor should be told to update it (e.g. \"via the merge\") to keep the rename consistent.\n\n## Strengths\n\n- Exceptionally well-anchored: every line-number citation (merge call 171, early return 189, struct end 52, carve-out 1351–1403, cap context close 1494, atomic-write context 1589, ExtractBody close 1747) was verified against the actual files and matches.\n- The carve-out rewrite is logically sound: I traced the second-write flow with the new guard — on-disk `trigger_count: 0` (via the inserted `writeTaskFile`) prevents the cap from re-firing and clearing `assignee: backtest-agent`, while `previous_assignee: claude` on disk is preserved because the incoming payload no longer carries the counter. This is exactly the reset the spec's Empty-to-Named Reset performs, and the spec's intent assertions are retained.\n- The claim that only the carve-out carries a differing on-disk/incoming counter is true (verified by reading every counter-bearing test block).\n- Container-autonomy is clean: no deploy/observe/kubectl, and the AC 14 git-diff check is correctly deferred to the host auditor — a real requirement here, since the daemon runs with `hideGit=true` (confirmed in `.dark-factory.log`).\n- Sibling-coverage and YAGNI passes are clean; failure modes and the spec's Security section are addressed.\n\n## Summary\n\nThis is a high-quality prompt: all code references verified correct, the test plan maps 1:1 to ACs 1–16 and 19, the carve-out is sound, and it is fully container-autonomous. One must-fix before approval: Requirement 8c ships no test that exercises the int/float64 numeric-equality path that the prompt's own context identifies as essential to DB 6 — the exact boundary-crossing gap the audit exists to catch. Add the two mixed-type `It` blocks above, and this is ready to ship.\n\nNote: I am audit-only and have not modified the prompt file. The find/replace pair in Critical Issue 1 is provided so you can apply it directly.","usage":{"total_tokens":0,"tool_uses":34,"duration_ms":374694},"uuid":"0f1c94c5-3cfc-4f27-aba6-f18bc5abb783","session_id":"d5b7e577-b8e1-45e9-9e5a-be2299ed6cef"}
